{{nihongo|'''Honda Shigetoki'''|本多重時}} (14 January 1583 – 12 November 1651) was the third [[shogun]] of the Honda Shogunate in Mitoyagi (modern-day [[Okaiken]]). The third son of the founding emperor [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]] and the younger half-brother of the second shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]], Shigetoki assumed power during the most volatile crisis of the early Honda period—the [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625, which claimed the lives of both his father and his half-brother within the span of twelve days. A scholarly bureaucrat rather than a warrior, Shigetoki transformed the catastrophe into the foundation of the {{nihongo|[[Pax Honda]]|パクス・ホンダ|Pakusu Honda}}—a 260-year peace that endured from 1625 until the [[Saito Restoration|Saito Restoration of 1871]].

Despite immense pressure from loyalist daimyos to completely eradicate the [[Ii clan]] for their role in the Emperor's assassination and the Shogun's death, Shigetoki made the highly controversial but ultimately stabilizing decision to grant them amnesty. This act, known as the [[Great Reconciliation]], prevented a second [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]] and secured over two centuries of relative peace under Honda rule. His reign was defined by the institutional consolidation of the shogunate, the relocation of the Imperial Court from [[Toyozawa]] to [[Okami]], the expansion of the [[Okami Rice Exchange]], and the commissioning of the ''[[Mitsukoji]]''—the official chronicle of the Sengoku period.

Historians widely consider Shigetoki to be the savior of the Honda Shogunate. Where his father had unified the nation through war and his brother had governed through force of personality, Shigetoki built the institutions that allowed the shogunate to survive without a great man at its helm. His decision to choose mercy over vengeance—guided by the mentorship of [[#The mentorship of Ikemoto Yousuke|Ikemoto Yousuke]], the last surviving member of the [[Honda Shitennō|Four Heavenly Pillars]]—remains one of the most consequential acts of statesmanship in Sanese history.

==Early life==

===Birth and family===

Shigetoki was born on 14 January 1583 in the castle town of [[Suemori]], a mountain district in [[Nagayama Prefecture]] on the island of [[Yagikoshu]]. His childhood name was {{nihongo|Naimon|内門}}. He was the third son of Honda Shigetada—then a vassal commander serving under [[Ogasawara Terumasa]]—and a low-ranking concubine named Lady Okyō.

Shigetoki's birth came just three months after that of his older half-brother, [[Honda Shigetomo]], who was born on 10 October 1582 to Shigetada's primary wife, [[Hatsu-no-kata]] (the daughter of the revered naval commander [[Anjō Motonobu]]). The proximity of their births meant the two boys grew up as near-twins, but the difference in their maternal lineage placed them on fundamentally different paths from the beginning. Shigetomo, born to the prestigious Anjō maternal line, was groomed from birth as the military heir to the Honda clan. Shigetoki, born to a concubine of no particular distinction, was never considered a primary candidate for the succession.

The eldest brother was [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Prince Takahito]] (the future Emperor Daigo), who was being raised separately for scholarly and ceremonial duties. Shigetoki thus occupied the third position in the family: after the imperial heir and the military heir. This position—neither the sword nor the throne—would prove to be the most consequential of the three, for it was the third son who would inherit the nation when the first two were taken.

===The scholarly upbringing===

While Shigetomo received rigorous martial training and was groomed for supreme command alongside their father—earning the nickname {{nihongo|"the Punisher"|お仕置き|Oshioki}}—Shigetoki was given a quieter, scholarly upbringing. He was tutored by [[Neo-Confucianism|Neo-Confucian]] scholars attached to the Honda household and received extensive education in classical literature, administrative law, political philosophy, and the economic theories that had been developed by his adoptive grandfather, [[Honda Shigetatsu]]. The curriculum reflected Shigetatsu's reformist legacy: the shift from the ''jofun'' rice tax to the ''kandaka'' coin system, the monetisation of the northern trade routes, and the promotion of competence over lineage.

This educational path was not a mark of disfavor but of strategic calculation. Shigetada, who had spent decades building the military machine that would unify the archipelago, understood that the next generation would need administrators as much as warriors. The transition from conquest to governance—the shift from the Sengoku period's culture of war to a culture of statecraft—required men who understood law, economics, and logistics. Shigetoki was trained for that world.

The young Shigetoki showed an early aptitude for bureaucratic work. He was meticulous, patient, and possessed of a temperament that stood in sharp contrast to his volatile half-brother. Where Shigetomo was volcanic—prone to rage, to impulsive action, to the dramatic gestures of a man raised on battlefields—Shigetoki was quiet, deliberate, and methodical. The ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' records that even as a boy, Shigetoki "preferred the abacus to the sword, and the ledger to the spear."

===Administrative apprenticeship===

Shigetoki spent much of his early adulthood in [[Okami]], the new capital that Shigetada had established as the political and administrative center of the shogunate in 1605. There, he was heavily exposed to the bureaucratic and logistical mechanisms of the new government. He observed the implementation of the [[Hokuto Omi–Shinshu road]] construction—a massive highway project connecting the northern provinces to the capital—and assisted in the management of the [[Okami Rice Exchange]], the centralized commodity market that Shigetomo had established in 1624 to standardize rice prices and convert rice taxes to currency.

This administrative background made Shigetoki a highly competent bureaucrat—preparing him for a crisis no one expected him to lead. While Shigetomo commanded armies and negotiated with daimyos, Shigetoki managed supply chains, audited tax records, and oversaw the infrastructure projects that kept the shogunate functioning. He was, in the words of the ''Mitsukoji'', "the man who counted the rice while others counted the dead."

===The shadow of the Sengoku===

Shigetoki grew up in a nation still bleeding from the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]]—158 years of civil war that had begun with the [[Taiju War]] in 1467. His father's military campaigns were the background of his childhood: the [[Battle of Tone]] in 1600, when 160,000 men fought in a single day and casualties may have reached 100,000; the [[Ishijima Highland Campaign]] of 1602–1604, which claimed the life of [[Ii Naomasa]]; the pacification campaigns that followed. By the time Shigetoki came of age, the warrior caste had been decimated, and the nation was exhausted in ways that only an administrator could fully appreciate.

The ceasefire that Shigetada had decreed after the Battle of Tone was not merely a political decision—it was an act of survival. The [[Yanbaru|Dragon Kingdom]] to the north and [[Nakamizu]] to the south were watching the archipelago bleed itself to death. If the Honda continued fighting, even in victory, the two maritime powers would invade a nation that had no soldiers left to defend itself. Shigetoki, trained in logistics and economics rather than warfare, understood this strategic reality more intuitively than the military men around him. He had seen the numbers: the depleted harvests, the empty granaries, the provinces that could no longer field the troops they had sent to Tone. The nation needed peace not as a luxury but as a necessity.

This understanding would shape every decision Shigetoki made when power was thrust upon him in 1625.

==The Kinzō Crisis of 1625==
{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}

The political landscape of Mitoyagi fractured in the summer of 1625. On 6 June, a coalition of disgruntled vassals led by {{nihongo|[[Ii Naotaka]]|井伊直孝}}—the son of Shigetada's most loyal vassal, [[Ii Naomasa]]—attacked the retirement palace at [[Toyozawa Castle|Toyozawa]], resulting in the death of Shigetoki's father, the Retired Emperor Shirakawa. The 69-year-old emperor, who had unified the archipelago and held both the imperial throne and the shogunate, was killed at the {{nihongo|"Bridge of Heaven"|天の橋|Ten no Hashi}} by a volley of matchlock fire—struck down by the very firearms he had championed at the Battle of Tone.

The assassination exploited the fatal flaw of the [[Dual Sovereignty]] system: the 500-kilometre gap between the Shogun's capital at Okami and the Retired Emperor's residence at Toyozawa. A massive typhoon severed all naval communication, and by the time Shogun Shigetomo learned of the attack, his father had been dead for nearly two days.

Driven by grief and rage, Shigetomo launched a massive, erratic retaliatory campaign against the Ii clan at [[Ichijōdani]]. On 18 June 1625, Shigetomo was mortally wounded outside the castle gates by an Ii musketeer—a shot fired despite Naotaka's explicit orders not to harm the Shogun. As Shigetomo lay dying in the mud, he pressed a bloodstained letter of forgiveness into Naotaka's hands and spoke his final word: {{nihongo|"Seal it"|封じろ|Fūjiro}}. Naotaka, overcome with grief, committed ''seppuku'' beside the dying Shogun, assisted by [[Hotta Muneshige]]. Within the span of twelve days, the Honda Shogunate had lost its two most powerful figureheads.

===Shigetoki thrust into command===

Shigetoki had originally been ordered by Shigetomo to move his soldiers 25 miles north of Ichijōdani to open a new front against the Ii clan's positions. When word reached him of his brother's death, the 42-year-old scholarly half-brother found himself the de facto leader of the military government. The situation was catastrophic: the imperial court was in shock, the main Honda armies were exhausted and leaderless at Ichijōdani, and several ''tozama'' (outer) daimyos were already preparing to rebel, sensing the imminent collapse of the Honda hegemony.

The full narrative of the Kinzō Incident—the assault on Toyozawa, Shigetomo's psychological deterioration, the siege of Ichijōdani, and the double death of Shigetomo and Naotaka—is treated in the dedicated article. For Shigetoki's purposes, the crisis presented an impossible set of simultaneous challenges: he had to stabilize the government, prevent a nationwide civil war, manage the grief and rage of the Honda loyalists, and somehow extract the nation from a cycle of vengeance that had consumed two generations—all while holding an army that was on the verge of dissolution.

==The mentorship of Ikemoto Yousuke==
{{Main|Honda Shitennō}}

The question that has occupied historians for centuries is: how did a scholarly bureaucrat with no military reputation manage to navigate the most dangerous crisis in the shogunate's history? The answer lies, in significant part, in the mentorship of {{nihongo|Ikemoto Yousuke|池本陽介}}—the last surviving member of the [[Honda Shitennō|Honda Shitennō]] (Four Heavenly Pillars), the four most distinguished military commanders who had served under Shigetada during the unification campaigns.

===The Fourth Pillar===

The Honda Shitennō were the military pillars of the Honda clan's rise to power. The traditional roster includes:

* '''[[Ii Naomasa]]''' (c. 1561–1604): The "Red Devil" (''Aka-oni''), the preeminent cavalry commander of the era, who died of an infected wound sustained at the [[Battle of Aonashiyama]] in 1604.
* '''[[Anjō Motonobu]]''': The naval commander who distinguished himself at the [[Battle of Yamanote]] (1599) and in the [[invasion of Ishijima]], and whose daughter Hatsu-no-kata became the mother of Honda Shigetomo.
* '''[[Sakakibara Yasumasa]]''': The siege warfare specialist who was killed leading an assault on the outer walls of Matsubara Castle during the [[Siege of Matsubara]] (1599–1600).
* The '''fourth member''' of the Shitennō varies by source. Some accounts list [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] in this position, though his later assassination of Ogasawara Terumasa and his death as an enemy of the Honda make this attribution controversial.

Modern scholarship, drawing on the declassified ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' archives and the correspondence of Hotta Muneshige, has identified the fourth Pillar as Ikemoto Yousuke (池本陽介)—a commander whose identity was less well-established in the popular tradition but whose role in the founding of the shogunate was, in the assessment of the Historiographical Bureau, "no less consequential than the other three."

===Yousuke's background===

Ikemoto Yousuke was a veteran of the unification campaigns who had served Shigetada from the earliest days of the Honda clan's rise. Unlike the other three Pillars—each of whom was defined by a single dramatic military specialty (cavalry, naval warfare, siege engineering)—Yousuke was a generalist: a strategist, an administrator, and a confidant. He had been present at the [[Battle of Tone]] and had served as a liaison between Shigetada and the imperial court during the delicate negotiations of 1601, when Shigetada's identity as the lost Prince Fumihito was formally recognized.

Yousuke's most important quality, however, was not his military record but his moral character. He had been one of the few men in Shigetada's inner circle who had witnessed the mercy lesson firsthand—the moment when the young Shigetada, having defeated the [[Minato clan]] at Ichijōdani (c. 1593–1594), dismissed a samurai who had killed [[Minato Yoshishige]] rather than rewarding him, uttering the words that would become the moral foundation of the Honda Shogunate:

{{quote|''"Not everything has to end by the blade."''|Honda Shigetada, ''Mitsukoji''}}

Yousuke had carried this lesson through the decades that followed. He had watched Shigetada apply it—sparing enemies, offering clemency, choosing restraint over annihilation—and he had watched the nation benefit from that restraint. He had also watched the cost of that restraint: the surviving enemies who became future threats, the resentment that festered in the hearts of men who had been spared but not forgiven. The mercy lesson, as Yousuke understood it, was not a simple virtue. It was a cost—a thing that must be paid for, sometimes with everything.

===The last surviving Pillar===

By the time of the Kinzō Incident in June 1625, the other three Pillars were already dead. Anjō Motonobu had died in battle. Sakakibara Yasumasa had fallen at the Siege of Matsubara. Ii Naomasa had died of his infected wound in 1604—his final words a warning to his son Naotaka about the dangers of leniency, a warning that would, two decades later, help drive Naotaka to assassinate the emperor his father had served.

Yousuke alone survived. He was an old man by 1625—past seventy, retired from active command, living in quiet retirement near the Heianji Temple in Okami. But when the news of the Kinzō Incident reached the capital, Yousuke presented himself at Okami Castle and offered his services to the new Shogun.

The relationship between Yousuke and Shigetoki was, in the framing of the ''Mitsukoji'', the critical transmission of wisdom from one generation to the next. Shigetoki had not been raised as a warrior. He had not fought at Tone. He had not witnessed the mercy lesson firsthand. He had not stood beside Shigetada when the nation was forged in blood. Yousuke had been there for all of it. And in the weeks following the Kinzō Incident, as Shigetoki struggled to decide between vengeance and clemency, Yousuke became the conduit through which the founding generation's wisdom reached the man who would have to act on it.

===The lessons Yousuke transmitted===

Yousuke's mentorship of Shigetoki, as recorded in the ''Mitsukoji'' and in the correspondence of Hotta Muneshige, centered on three principles:

====The mercy lesson====

Yousuke taught Shigetoki the full weight of the mercy lesson—not as a simple injunction to be kind, but as a strategic philosophy. Shigetada had taught that restraint was not weakness but a deeper form of strength. Yousuke had watched this principle in action for decades. He had seen Shigetada spare enemies who became loyal vassals, and he had seen Shigetada's mercy fail when the spared enemy was Ii Naotaka. The lesson, as Yousuke transmitted it, was not that mercy always works, but that mercy is the only option that does not guarantee destruction. Annihilation of the Ii clan would require years of grinding warfare that the fragile, post-unification economy could not sustain. It would alienate every ''daimyō'' who feared he might be next. It would invite the external powers—Yanbaru to the north, Nakamizu to the south—to invade a nation that had no soldiers left to defend itself. Mercy was not merely moral; it was the only strategic option that did not guarantee the collapse of the state.

====The cost of war====

Yousuke had been present at the Battle of Tone. He had seen the "Crimson Fog"—the blood-and-dust haze that obscured the entire battlefield. He had watched 100,000 men die in a single day. He had spent the subsequent decades managing the consequences: the depleted harvests, the empty provinces, the warrior caste that had been bled white. When the Honda loyalists demanded the annihilation of the Ii clan, Yousuke's counsel to Shigetoki was rooted not in theory but in memory: "I have seen what happens when a nation chooses the blade. I have spent twenty-five years counting the cost. Do not make me count it again."

====The founding generation's intent====

Yousuke was one of the last men alive who had known Shigetada personally— who had served beside him, who had heard his private thoughts, who had understood not just what the founder had done but why he had done it. Shigetada's purpose, as Yousuke understood it, was not to build a military empire but to build a nation that could survive without war. The Dual Sovereignty system, the Kinzō Reforms, the Okami Rice Exchange—all of these were instruments of a single purpose: the transition from a nation ruled by the sword to a nation ruled by law. Yousuke taught Shigetoki that this purpose could not be fulfilled through vengeance. To annihilate the Ii clan would be to admit that the nation was still ruled by the sword—that the founding generation's dream of a nation governed by law rather than by blood was a failure. Mercy was not a departure from Shigetada's vision; it was its fulfillment.

===How Yousuke shaped Shigetoki's decision===

The decision to spare the Ii clan was not made in a single moment. It was the product of weeks of deliberation, during which Shigetoki was pulled in two directions: the Honda loyalists, led by the [[Hotta clan]], demanded absolute vengeance; Yousuke and the surviving members of the founding generation counseled restraint. The bloodstained letters found on Shigetomo's body—offering forgiveness to Naotaka and the Higo domain to the Ii clan—provided the moral and legal foundation for the amnesty. But it was Yousuke's mentorship that gave Shigetoki the moral courage to act on that foundation.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records Yousuke's counsel to Shigetoki in a passage that has become one of the most quoted in Sanese historical literature:

{{quote|Your father built a nation. Your brother tried to govern it. Both of them died for it. Now you must decide: will you be the man who saves what they built, or the man who destroys it in their name? The blade that cuts the cycle of revenge is infinitely heavier than the blade that continues it. Your father knew this. Your brother learned it too late. You have the chance to learn it in time.|Ikemoto Yousuke to Honda Shigetoki, ''Mitsukoji''}}

Shigetoki chose the heavier blade. The Great Reconciliation was the result.

==The Great Reconciliation==
{{Main|Great Reconciliation|Siege of Ichijoudani}}

The immediate expectation among the Honda retainers, particularly those of the [[Hotta clan]] who had fought beside Shigetomo, was absolute vengeance. The council of elders demanded that Shigetoki mobilize the remaining reserves, march on Ichijōdani, and execute every man, woman, and child bearing the Ii name. The fury was not merely political; it was personal. Shigetomo had been loved by his men. His death—shot in the mud outside the castle gates while carrying a letter of forgiveness—was felt as a desecration.

===The decision to spare the Ii clan===

Shigetoki recognized that giving in to this bloodlust would spark a nationwide civil war. The Ii clan still commanded immense loyalty among the ''fudai'' (hereditary vassal) houses, and annihilating them would require years of grinding warfare that the fragile, post-unification economy could not sustain. Furthermore, to the south, the nation of [[Nakamizu]] had recently been reunified by the [[Kawaei clan]] under [[Kawaei Yoshiyuki]], who had deployed over 80,000 soldiers in April 1620 and another 25,000 in March 1621. The Honda Shogunate could not afford to execute its most capable generals while a reunified southern neighbor was aggressively expanding.

His decision was heavily influenced by the recovery of the bloodstained "Ii Letters"—the final correspondence found on Shigetomo's body. In these letters, Shigetomo had expressed profound regret over his own erratic actions and a dying wish to forgive Naotaka to preserve the peace. The letter offered Naotaka the [[Higo Province|Higo domain]]—a territory in the deep south, as far from the violence as geography would allow—and full pardon for the assassination of Emperor Shirakawa. It was the second of two letters Shigetomo had written: the first, found crumpled and unsent, was wildly aggressive; the second was a complete reversal in tone, addressing Naotaka with profound vulnerability and offering forgiveness.

The bloodstained letter served as the legal and moral foundation for the amnesty: proof that the Shogun himself had forgiven the Ii before his death. Combined with the counsel of Ikemoto Yousuke and the strategic imperative of avoiding civil war, it gave Shigetoki the authority to act.

===The Edict of Clemency===

In late July 1625, Shigetoki shocked the nation by issuing the [[Edict of Clemency]]. Instead of ordering the destruction of the Ii clan, Shigetoki officially pardoned them. He declared that the deaths of his father and brother were tragic consequences of political misunderstandings rather than malicious treason. He negotiated a settlement wherein the Ii clan was allowed to keep their territory in Ichijōdani, though they were stripped of 70,000 ''koku'' to pay for war reparations and the construction of memorial temples.

The southern half of Ichijōdani was retained by the Ii; the northern half was surrendered to the Shogunate. Naotaka's son, [[Ii Naoyoshi]], accepted the terms of surrender and ensured the Ii clan became one of the most staunchly loyal political forces during the remainder of the Honda Period. The clan's survival—spared when annihilation was expected, forgiven when vengeance was demanded—transformed the Ii from a rebel house into a symbol of the Shogunate's capacity for mercy.

===The role of Emperor Daigo===

Shigetoki did not act alone. [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]], isolated in the Okami Imperial Palace, had suddenly found himself as the senior surviving member of his immediate family. The realm was on the brink of total collapse, with the military exhausted and regional lords preparing to resume the Sengoku wars. In this critical moment, Daigo utilized his supreme spiritual authority to stabilize the government.

He formally invested Shigetoki as the third Shogun on 10 July 1625. Together, the two surviving brothers made the difficult, deeply unpopular decision to forgo vengeance. Daigo gave his imperial blessing to the Edict of Clemency, and his public declaration that the cycle of bloodshed had taken enough of his family and must end was instrumental in forcing the furious Honda loyalists to stand down.

===The political settlement===

The Great Reconciliation was not merely an amnesty. It was a comprehensive political settlement that restructured the shogunate. Its key provisions included:

* '''Amnesty for the Ii clan''': The Ii were stripped of territory and revenue but spared annihilation—a revolutionary act in an era where defeated enemies were typically erased.
* '''Relocation of the Imperial Court''': The Imperial Court was permanently moved from Toyozawa to Okami, ending the 500-kilometre geographic split that had enabled the Kinzō Incident.
* '''Formal establishment of Pax Honda''': The settlement established the framework for the 260-year peace that would follow.
* '''Commission of the ''Mitsukoji''''': Emperor Daigo commissioned the official chronicle of the Sengoku period, ensuring that the history of the era—and the cost of the peace—was recorded for posterity.

This act of profound pragmatism, later dubbed the Great Reconciliation, successfully defused the crisis. By absorbing the emotional blow of his family's deaths for the sake of the state, Shigetoki won the deep, enduring respect of both the ''fudai'' and ''tozama'' daimyos. The Ii clan, indebted to Shigetoki's mercy, became the most staunchly loyal defenders of the Honda Shogunate for the next two centuries.

==Founding Pax Honda==

The Great Reconciliation was the founding act of {{nihongo|Pax Honda|パクス・ホンダ|Pakusu Honda}}—the 260-year peace that followed the Sengoku period. The restructuring of the Shogunate under Honda Shigetoki ended the geographic split of Dual Sovereignty, consolidated power in Okami, and established the framework for a peace that would last until 1871.

===The vision of a nation at peace===

Shigetoki's vision for Pax Honda was shaped by the lessons of the Sengoku period and the mentorship of Ikemoto Yousuke. The founding generation—Shigetada, Naomasa, Yousuke, and the other Pillars—had unified the nation through war. But they had always understood that war was the means, not the end. The end was a nation that could survive without war: a nation governed by law, sustained by commerce, and united by institutions rather than by the personality of a single great man.

Shigetoki's contribution to this vision was institutional. Where his father had ruled through charisma and military genius, and his brother had governed through force of personality and martial terror, Shigetoki sought to build systems that could survive weaker rulers. He understood—perhaps better than anyone in his family—that the shogunate could not depend on producing another Shigetada or another Shigetomo. It had to be able to function with an ordinary man at its head. This meant institutions, not personalities; laws, not edicts; precedents, not improvisations.

===The institutional framework===

The Pax Honda was built on several institutional foundations that Shigetoki established or strengthened during his reign:

* '''The consolidation of sovereignty''': By relocating the Imperial Court to Okami, Shigetoki eliminated the geographic vulnerability that had enabled the Kinzō Incident. The emperor and the shogun now resided in the same city, within the same castle complex. The Dual Sovereignty system, in its original form, was effectively dissolved—replaced by a unified sovereignty in which the emperor held spiritual authority and the shogun held practical authority, but both operated from a single location.
* '''The bureaucratic apparatus''': Shigetoki expanded and formalized the administrative system that Shigetomo had begun. Provincial governors were subject to regular review and transfer. The inspectorate (''metsuke'') was strengthened to monitor the daimyos. The legal code was systematized, replacing the patchwork of local customs with a unified framework.
* '''The economic infrastructure''': The Okami Rice Exchange, established by Shigetomo in 1624, was expanded and stabilized under Shigetoki's direct supervision. The road network—initiated with the Hokuto Omi–Shinshu road—was extended, providing the infrastructure for both commerce and governance.
* '''The cultural patronage''': Shigetoki heavily subsidized the arts, leading to a flourishing of early modern Sanese culture. This was not merely generosity; it was a deliberate policy of redirecting the energy of the warrior class from warfare to cultural pursuits. The samurai who had once defined themselves by the sword were encouraged to define themselves by the brush, the tea ceremony, and the poetic tradition.

==Reign as Shogun==

Following the resolution of the Kinzō crisis, Shigetoki was officially invested as the third Shogun on 10 July 1625 by his brother, [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]]. The investiture was a quiet affair—deliberately so. Shigetoki refused the elaborate ceremonies that had accompanied his father's ascension to the throne and his brother's investiture as Shogun. The nation was in mourning, the treasury was depleted, and the army was exhausted. The message was clear: the era of grand gestures was over. The era of governance had begun.

Shigetoki's twenty-six-year tenure (1625–1651) was characterized by a shift from military expansion to bureaucratic consolidation. Recognizing that his father and brother had ruled through sheer force of personality and martial terror, Shigetoki sought to institutionalize the shogunate's power so it could survive weaker rulers. This was, in many ways, his greatest achievement: he built a system that did not require a genius to operate it.

===Governance philosophy===

Shigetoki's governance philosophy was shaped by three formative influences:

# '''The Neo-Confucian education''': His scholarly training had instilled in him a deep respect for institutional design, legal codes, and the Confucian ideal of the ruler as a moral exemplar who governs through propriety rather than force.
# '''The administrative apprenticeship''': His years managing the Okami Rice Exchange and overseeing infrastructure projects had given him practical experience in the mechanics of governance—taxation, logistics, personnel management, and the importance of reliable data.
# '''The mentorship of Ikemoto Yousuke''': Yousuke's transmission of the founding generation's wisdom had given Shigetoki a strategic framework for understanding the purpose of the shogunate—not as a military dictatorship but as a vehicle for the transition from war to governance.

The result was a governance style that was deliberate, methodical, and consensus-oriented. Where Shigetomo had issued edicts and expected immediate compliance, Shigetoki consulted his council, built coalitions, and accepted slower progress in exchange for broader support. Where Shigetada had ruled through the sheer authority of his personal legend, Shigetoki ruled through the authority of institutions.

The ''kikungenpo''—the collection of [[Hotta Muneshige]]'s political philosophies compiled in the final volume of the ''Mitsukoji''—articulates the principle that guided Shigetoki's governance:

{{quote|The blade that cuts the cycle of revenge is infinitely heavier than the blade that continues it. A general wields the latter; a Shogun must wield the former.|the ''kikungenpo'', on the pardon of the Ii clan}}

Shigetoki wielded the heavier blade—not once, on the battlefield, but every day, in the council chamber. The discipline of mercy, maintained over twenty-six years, was a greater feat of strength than any single act of battlefield valor.

===Slowing the Kinzō Reforms===

One of Shigetoki's first acts as Shogun was to slow the aggressive implementation of the [[Kinzō Reforms]] that had triggered the initial rebellion. The reforms—authored by Emperor Shirakawa and enforced by Shigetomo—had sought to dismantle the traditional land-ownership rights of the samurai class through a national land survey, the establishment of the Okami Rice Exchange, and the proposed abolition of swords. These reforms had been viewed by the conservative warrior caste as an existential threat.

Shigetoki did not abandon the reforms. He recognized, as his father had, that the transition from a rice-based economy to a currency-based mercantile economy was necessary for the long-term survival of the state. But he opted for a gradual transition of land rights that appeased the conservative samurai class. The most controversial provisions—the Abolition of Swords, in particular—were softened or deferred. The National Land Survey was continued, but at a pace that allowed local lords to adjust rather than resist.

This approach was characteristic of Shigetoki's governance: the destination remained the same, but the pace was calibrated to avoid provoking the very rebellion that the reforms had originally triggered. The ''Mitsukoji'' records Shigetoki's explanation to his council: "My brother tried to cross the river in a single stride and was swept away. I shall cross it in two strides and arrive dry."

===Bureaucratic consolidation===

Shigetoki's most enduring contribution to the shogunate was the institutionalization of its administrative apparatus. He inherited a government that had been built around the personalities of Shigetada and Shigetomo—men who could issue orders and expect them to be followed because of who they were. Shigetoki, who lacked their personal authority, had to build institutions that commanded obedience through their structure rather than through the charisma of the man at their head.

He expanded the role of the provincial governors, establishing a system of regular review and transfer that prevented any single governor from building an independent power base. He strengthened the inspectorate (''metsuke''), the shogunate's intelligence network, which monitored the daimyos for signs of disloyalty or independent ambition. He systematized the legal code, replacing the patchwork of local customs and ad hoc edicts with a unified framework that applied consistently across the archipelago.

He also formalized the system of Alternate Attendance (''sankin-kōtai''), which required daimyos to spend alternating years in the capital, ensuring that they could not build independent military forces in their home domains while their wives and heirs served as effective hostages in Okami. This system, which Shigetoki institutionalized and his successor [[Honda Shigemasa|Shigemasa]] would weaponize, became one of the most effective tools of shogunal control during the Pax Honda.

===Economic reforms===

Shigetoki's economic policy centered on the expansion and stabilization of the [[Okami Rice Exchange]], the centralized commodity market that Shigetomo had established in 1624. The Exchange allowed daimyos and merchants to trade in rice futures—contracts for rice to be delivered at a later date—stabilizing the price of rice and reducing the economic shock of bad harvests. Under Shigetoki's direct supervision, the Exchange was expanded to handle a wider range of commodities, and its regulatory framework was strengthened to prevent the speculation and fraud that had plagued its early years.

The road network initiated under Shigetomo—the [[Hokuto Omi–Shinshu road]], which connected the northern provinces to the capital—was extended and maintained under Shigetoki's reign. The roads served a dual purpose: they facilitated commerce by allowing goods to move efficiently across the archipelago, and they facilitated governance by allowing officials, messengers, and troops to move quickly in response to any disturbance.

Shigetoki also maintained the strict separation of the Imperial Court and the military government that his father had established. Emperor Daigo remained entirely focused on ceremonial and religious duties while the ''bakufu'' handled statecraft. This separation, which had been a source of tension under the Dual Sovereignty system, became a source of stability under Shigetoki's unified sovereignty: the emperor provided spiritual legitimacy, the shogun provided practical governance, and neither encroached on the other's domain.

===Cultural patronage===

Shigetoki heavily subsidized the arts, leading to a flourishing of early modern Sanese culture. This was not merely a matter of personal taste—though Shigetoki, unlike his martial half-brother, was genuinely interested in literature, poetry, and the tea ceremony—but a deliberate policy of cultural transformation. The samurai class, which had defined itself through warfare for over a century, needed a new identity in an era of peace. By subsidizing the arts, Shigetoki encouraged the samurai to redirect their energy from the sword to the brush, from the battlefield to the tea room.

The result was the emergence of a distinctive urban culture centered on Okami and the castle towns of the provincial governors. This culture—characterized by its emphasis on aesthetics, literature, and the tea ceremony—was a product of the peace and prosperity that the Honda Shogunate provided. It represented a significant departure from the martial culture of the Sengoku era and laid the foundation for the cultural flowering that would define the Pax Honda.

Among Shigetoki's personal cultural interests was a fascination with {{nihongo|Shisa|シーサー}}, the traditional Yanbarun guardian beast statues. Following the Maritime Accords with Yanbaru in the 1630s, Shigetoki commissioned hundreds of Shisa for his capital, adopting the guardian figures as a symbol of the cross-archipelago cultural bond. The Shisa was exported throughout Okaiken and to Sanese enclaves abroad, becoming an enduring cultural icon.

==Relocating the Imperial Court to Okami==
{{Main|Dual Sovereignty}}

The Kinzō Incident had proven that the 500-kilometre split between Okami and Toyozawa was a strategic impossibility. The "Tyranny of Distance"—the communication delay that had prevented the Shogun from protecting the Emperor—was the fatal flaw of the Dual Sovereignty system. [[Ii Naotaka]] had calculated his entire strategy around the time lag: by the time the Shogun in Okami learned of the assault on Toyozawa, the Emperor was already dead.

Following the Great Reconciliation, Shigetoki made the decision to permanently relocate the Imperial Court from Toyozawa to Okami. The construction of the [[Okami Imperial Palace]]—within the walls of Okami Castle, which Shigetada had built decades earlier—consolidated the imperial and military authorities in a single location for the first time. Toyozawa's status as a capital city ended.

This decision was one of the most consequential of Shigetoki's reign. It had three major effects:

# '''Strategic security''': The Emperor was now within the protective walls of Okami Castle, guarded by the Shogun's own forces. The geographic vulnerability that had enabled the Kinzō Incident was eliminated. No future rebel could exploit the distance between the two sovereigns, because there was no distance.
# '''Political consolidation''': The relocation effectively dissolved the Dual Sovereignty system in its original form. The emperor and the shogun now operated from the same city, within the same complex. The spiritual and military authorities, while still formally distinct, were now physically and institutionally unified. This consolidation strengthened the shogunate's control over the imperial court and eliminated the possibility of the court pursuing an independent political agenda.
# '''Symbolic significance''': The relocation symbolized the end of the Sengoku period's political geography. The era of competing capitals—of military capitals and imperial capitals, of northern power centers and southern power centers—was over. Okami was now the sole capital of the realm, the center of both spiritual and military authority. The nation had been physically unified.

The relocation was not without controversy. The court nobles (''kuge'') of Toyozawa, who had enjoyed a degree of independence and influence under the Dual Sovereignty system, resisted the move. But Shigetoki's authority, reinforced by Emperor Daigo's cooperation, was sufficient to carry the decision. The ''kuge'' were relocated to Okami, where they were accommodated in a new palace complex within the castle walls—and where they were, effectively, under the shogunate's direct supervision.

The end of Toyozawa as a capital was also the end of an era. The city that had served as the imperial residence under the Dual Sovereignty system—the "Palace of the Sea God" from which Emperor Shirakawa had guided the grand strategy of the realm—was reduced to a provincial town. Its castle, once the seat of the Retired Emperor, was abandoned. The burning of Toyozawa Castle during the Kinzō Incident and the subsequent relocation of the court meant that the city that had been the spiritual capital of the unified nation was now a ruin—a monument to the cost of the Sengoku period's final tragedy.

==Commissioning the Mitsukoji==
{{Main|Mitsukoji}}

One of Shigetoki's most enduring legacies was his role in the commissioning of the ''[[Mitsukoji]]''—the official chronicle of the Sengoku period and the Honda clan's rise to power. The chronicle was commissioned by Emperor Daigo in the 1640s, during the final decades of his reign, as part of the Great Reconciliation's program of historical documentation.

===Emperor Daigo's vision===

Daigo, who had guided the imperial court through the Kinzō Incident and the Great Reconciliation, recognized that the events of his family's rise—the unification wars, the founding of the Shogunate, and the tragedy of 1625—risked being lost to myth, partisan distortion, or oblivion. He personally oversaw the early stages of the compilation, ensuring that the history of his family's rise and the tragedies of 1625 were recorded for posterity.

The ''Mitsukoji'' records Daigo's commissioning instruction in a preface attributed to the emperor himself:

{{quote|Let the record be honest. Let it say what was done and what was felt. Let it not flatter the living or defame the dead. Let the children of this land read it and know what their peace cost.|Emperor Daigo, commissioning instruction for the ''Mitsukoji''}}

===Shigetoki's role===

While the ''Mitsukoji'' was formally commissioned by Emperor Daigo, Shigetoki's role in the project was substantial. As Shogun, he provided the institutional support—the funding, the personnel, and the access to archives—that made the compilation possible. The Shogunate Historiographical Bureau, established specifically for this purpose, drew on primary sources that were still available in the mid-17th century: battlefield reports, personal correspondence, the testimony of surviving retainers, and the records of the various Honda branch families.

Shigetoki also ensured that the bureau had access to the surviving members of the founding generation. [[Hotta Muneshige]], who had served as Shigetomo's second-in-command throughout the siege and had been present at the double death at Ichijōdani, contributed his political philosophies—compiled in the ''kikungenpo'' chapter of the chronicle. Other surviving retainers provided testimony that was cross-referenced with the documentary record.

The decision to archive the completed chronicle in the [[Heianji Temple]] rather than a government office was deliberate: the temple was a neutral space, outside the direct control of the Shogunate's political apparatus, which gave the chronicle a degree of institutional independence from the very regime it documented.

===The vision of historical documentation===

Shigetoki's support for the ''Mitsukoji'' reflected his understanding that the Pax Honda could not endure without a shared historical narrative. The nation had been forged in blood and unified through mercy; but the memory of that blood and that mercy would fade within a generation if it were not recorded. The ''Mitsukoji'' was, in this sense, not merely a chronicle but a foundation document for the peace—a text that would remind future generations what their peace had cost and why it must be maintained.

The chronicle was completed in 1680—nearly thirty years after Shigetoki's death—and served as the official history of the era for over three centuries. It organized the narrative of the Sengoku period around the key concepts that Shigetoki's generation had established: the [[#The Stone Pounding Cycle|Stone Pounding Cycle]], the Dual Sovereignty system, the mercy lesson, and the Pax Honda.

==Negotiations with Yanbaru==
{{Main|Okaiken-Yanbaru relations}}

The most consequential early modern interaction between the Honda Shogunate and the [[Yanbaru|Yanbaru Dragon Kingdom]] occurred during the 1625 succession crisis. Following the death of Shogun Honda Shigetomo, Yanbaru—alongside [[Nakamizu]]—deployed warships near the Sea of Mitoyagi, probing for territorial concessions. The Yanbarun ports teemed with displaced Sanese mercenaries eager to exploit the chaos of the Kinzō Incident.

===Crisis management===

Shigetoki and his regent, [[Hotta Muneshige]], averted conflict through a masterclass in crisis management. The approach combined diplomatic conciliation with a credible display of military capability—a strategy that reflected Shigetoki's governance philosophy of achieving through institutions what others achieved through force.

Muneshige dispatched a high-level condolence embassy to [[Yomitan]], the Yanbarun capital, formally notifying the Yanbarun royal court of the deaths of Emperor Shirakawa and Shogun Shigetomo and requesting Yanbaru's restraint during the succession. The embassy carried gifts and a personal letter from Shigetoki emphasizing the shared cultural heritage of the two nations and the new Shogun's commitment to maintaining stable relations.

Simultaneously, Shigetoki opened the lucrative Mitoyagi rice futures market to Yanbarun merchants—a significant economic concession that gave Yanbaru a direct financial stake in the stability of the Honda Shogunate. By allowing Yanbarun traders to participate in the Okami Rice Exchange, Shigetoki created an economic interdependence that made military confrontation against Okaiken's interests.

To back up the diplomacy with deterrence, the Shogunate conducted massive naval exercises in the straits separating Yagikoshu from the Yanbaru archipelago. The exercises, involving the Anjō-Murakami Suigun (the shogunate's naval forces), signaled that the Honda military, despite the trauma of the Kinzō Incident, remained capable of defending the northern maritime border.

===The Maritime Accords of Okami===

Shigetoki then negotiated a lesser alliance marriage with the Yanbarun royal family, permanently securing the northern maritime border. The resulting {{nihongo|Maritime Accords of Okami|岡見海事協定}} (c. 1630s) transformed the relationship between the two nations.

The accords allowed Sanese merchants to bypass heavy [[Zong]] tariffs by chartering Yanbarun vessels, making Yanbaru the singular loophole that integrated Okaiken's closed economy into the global trade network during the isolationist ''[[Kaikin]]'' period that would follow under Shigetoki's successor. Yanbaru became the primary catalyst for the early Sanese diaspora, facilitating the emigration of displaced samurai to the Zong colony of [[Tamsui]] in [[Lurandia]].

The Maritime Accords also triggered a massive cultural export. Shigetoki became fascinated by {{nihongo|Shisa|シーサー}}, the traditional Yanbarun guardian beast statues, commissioning hundreds for his capital. The Shisa was adopted throughout Okaiken and exported to Sanese enclaves in Lurandia, becoming a symbol of the cross-archipelago cultural bond that persists to the present day.

The successful resolution of the Yanbaru crisis was one of Shigetoki's most significant diplomatic achievements. It demonstrated that the shogunate, even in its most vulnerable moment, could manage external threats through a combination of diplomacy, economic incentives, and credible deterrence—without firing a single shot.

==The Stone Pounding Cycle==
{{Main|Mythology of Okaiken}}

The Great Reconciliation and the founding of Pax Honda are often interpreted through the framework of the {{nihongo|Stone Pounding Cycle|石打ちの輪|Ishi-uchi no Wa}}, a mythological concept from the [[Mythology of Okaiken|mythology of Okaiken]] that describes the cyclical nature of history through three phases:

# '''Pounding''' (''Uchi''): The phase of warfare, conflict, and the hardening of institutional structures. The Sengoku period, with its 158 years of civil war, was the ultimate Pounding phase. The Honda clan's military campaigns—from Mazama to Tone—were the pounding of the stone, the violent process of forging a unified state from the fragmented archipelago.
# '''Loosening''' (''Yurumi''): The phase of collapse, when the hard institutional crust breaks down and the soft human reality beneath is exposed. The Kinzō Incident—the assassination of Emperor Shirakawa, the psychological deterioration of Shogun Shigetomo, the siege of Ichijōdani—was the Loosening, the moment when the ''tetsu-kage'' (the "iron shadow" of institutional design) cracked and the human frailty beneath was revealed.
# '''Release''' (''Kai''): The phase of unification and gratitude, when the cycle is completed and a new order is established through forgiveness rather than force. The Great Reconciliation, with its amnesty for the Ii clan and its foundation in Shigetomo's mercy, was the Release—the moment when the cycle of violence was sealed and a new era of peace began.

Shigetoki's role in this framework was that of the Release—the man who completed the cycle by choosing mercy over vengeance. Where his father had been the Pounding (the warrior who forged the nation through war) and his brother had been the Loosening (the man whose grief and rage cracked the institutions his father had built), Shigetoki was the Release: the man who sealed the cycle and established the peace.

The Stone Pounding Cycle is also mapped onto the {{nihongo|Niō|仁王}} Guardians—the two fierce protectors of Buddhist temples who stand at the gate:

* '''Agyō''' (the Open-Mouthed Guardian): The active, dynamic principle, associated with Pounding and with the Shogun. The Shogun, as the military and administrative authority, was the Agyō—the one who acts, who pounds, who enforces.
* '''Ungyō''' (the Closed-Mouthed Guardian): The still, receptive principle, associated with Release and with the Emperor. The Emperor, as the spiritual and ceremonial authority, was the Ungyō—the one who receives, who seals, who releases.

In this framework, Shigetoki's reign represented the transition from the Agyō phase (the active, martial governance of Shigetada and Shigetomo) to the Ungyō phase (the receptive, consolidating governance of Shigetoki). His governance style—quiet, institutional, consensus-oriented—was the embodiment of the Ungyō principle: the stillness that follows the storm, the silence that follows the pounding.

==Later years==

===The aging Shogun===

As Shigetoki aged, the shogunate he had built began to show the first signs of the tensions that would define its later history. The peace he had established was genuine, but it was maintained by a delicate balance of institutional constraints, economic incentives, and the memory of the Sengoku period's horrors. As that memory faded—as the generation that had fought at Tone and Ichijōdani passed away—the constraints that held the daimyos in check began to weaken.

Shigetoki's response to these tensions was characteristic: he strengthened the institutions rather than relying on personal authority. He expanded the inspectorate, tightened the Alternate Attendance system, and reinforced the economic dependence of the daimyos on the Okami Rice Exchange. These measures were sufficient to maintain the peace during his lifetime, but they stored up tensions that would be released—explosively—under his successor.

===The succession question===

Shigetoki had no direct male heir of his own body who was considered suitable for the succession. To ensure continuity, he had previously adopted Shigetomo's second son, [[Honda Tadamasa]], to ensure his own branch family's continuation. However, the main shogunal line was to pass back to the primary lineage: Shigetoki designated Shigetomo's eldest son, [[Honda Shigemasa]], as his successor.

This decision was politically significant. By passing the shogunate back to Shigetomo's eldest son, Shigetoki affirmed the principle that the shogunal line belonged to the primary lineage—the descendants of Shigetomo and Lady Saigō—rather than to his own branch. This principle would guide the succession for the remainder of the Pax Honda, with the shogunate passing through the Okami branch and, when direct heirs were lacking, through cadet branches such as the Yamanaka and Fujisato lines.

The Fujisato branch—descended from Shigetoki himself—would eventually provide the final Shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], who was adopted into the main line in the mid-19th century and would preside over the peaceful dissolution of the shogunate during the [[Saito Restoration]].

===The growing shadow of Shigemasa===

Historical records indicate that Shigemasa deeply resented his uncle's pragmatic mercy. The young man—born in 1605, twenty years old at the time of the Kinzō Incident—viewed the Great Reconciliation as a profound insult to his father's memory. He spent the twenty-six years of Shigetoki's reign quietly building a network of fiercely loyal, hardline military retainers within Okami Castle, biding his time.

Shigetoki was aware of his nephew's resentment but chose not to confront it directly. His governance philosophy emphasized institutional constraints over personal confrontation, and he may have believed that the system he had built would be sufficient to channel Shigemasa's ambitions within acceptable bounds. In this, he was mistaken. The system he built was strong enough to maintain the peace, but not strong enough to prevent his successor from dismantling its conciliatory character.

==Death and succession==

Shigetoki died peacefully of natural causes at [[Okami Castle]] on 12 November 1651 at the age of 68. His death, in stark contrast to the violent deaths of his father and brother, was a testament to the peace he had established. The man who had inherited a nation in flames passed away in a nation at rest.

He was succeeded by Shigetomo's eldest son, [[Honda Shigemasa]], seamlessly passing power back to the primary lineage. The succession was orderly and uncontested—a remarkable achievement in a nation that had been defined, for over a century, by succession crises and civil wars. The institutional framework that Shigetoki had built functioned as designed: the transfer of power was smooth, the daimyos accepted the new Shogun, and the peace held.

The 46-year-old Shigemasa immediately moved to consolidate absolute power and dismantle the conciliatory political structure his uncle had built. Firmly believing that clemency bred weakness and that the only way to prevent another Kinzō Incident was through overwhelming fear, Shigemasa instigated the Keian Purges, implemented draconian nationwide cadastral surveys, brutally suppressed all non-Baiduist religions, and enacted the Great Seclusion (''[[Kaikin]]'') laws. These policies effectively sealed Mitoyagi into a technological and cultural time capsule, guaranteeing the absolute survival of the Honda Shogunate for over two centuries but severely stunting the nation's global development.

The contrast between Shigetoki and Shigemasa—between the open hand and the iron fist—would define the historiography of the Pax Honda. Traditionalist historians argue that Shigemasa's ruthless centralization was a grim necessity that completed the work Shigetoki had begun. Revisionist scholars contend that Shigemasa's authoritarianism destroyed the conciliatory, institutional character of Shigetoki's governance and replaced it with a system that could only survive through terror. Both perspectives acknowledge that the Pax Honda was the product of both men: Shigetoki's mercy established the peace, and Shigemasa's terror maintained it.

==Legacy==

===The savior of the Honda Shogunate===

While he is rarely depicted in historical dramas with the same dramatic flair as his legendary father or his volatile half-brother, historians widely consider Shigetoki to be the savior of the Honda Shogunate. Without his cool-headed intervention and his willingness to forego personal vengeance, the unified Mitoyagi state would almost certainly have collapsed back into the warring chaos of the Sengoku period.

Shigetoki's achievement was not merely the Great Reconciliation—though that alone would have secured his place in history. His deeper achievement was the institutional transformation of the shogunate from a personal dictatorship, dependent on the charisma and military genius of a single man, into a bureaucratic state capable of surviving ordinary rulers. The Pax Honda endured for 260 years not because the Honda clan produced a succession of great shoguns—it did not—but because Shigetoki built a system that did not require greatness to function.

===The founder of the peace===

The {{nihongo|Pax Honda|パクス・ホンダ|Pakusu Honda}}—the 260-year peace that followed the Sengoku period—was the longest sustained peace in Sanese history and one of the longest in world history. It was founded on Shigetoki's decision to spare the Ii clan, consolidated by his institutional reforms, and maintained by the system he built. The peace was not perfect—it was maintained, in its later decades, by increasingly authoritarian measures—but it was real. For 260 years, the Sanese archipelago was free from major internal warfare, allowing culture, commerce, and agriculture to flourish.

Shigetoki's role as the founder of this peace is his most enduring legacy. The ''Mitsukoji'' frames the Great Reconciliation not as a victory but as a sacrifice: "The nation was not conquered into peace—it was forgiven into peace." Shigetoki was the man who chose forgiveness. He was the man who absorbed the grief and rage of his family's deaths and transformed them into the foundation of a peace that would outlast him by two centuries.

===The Fujisato branch===

Shigetoki's descendants formed the {{nihongo|Fujisato branch|藤里 branch}} of the Honda clan. While the main shogunal line passed through Shigetomo's eldest son Shigemasa, the Fujisato line continued through Shigetoki's adopted son Tadamasa and his biological son Shigenori. The Fujisato branch would eventually provide the final Shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], who was adopted into the main line in the mid-19th century and presided over the peaceful dissolution of the shogunate during the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871.

Yoshinobu's decision to voluntarily return administrative power to Emperor Saito—a peaceful transition that avoided the bloodshed that had accompanied the end of many historical regimes—was widely praised as an act of statesmanship. In a sense, Yoshinobu's peaceful surrender of power echoed Shigetoki's peaceful assumption of it: both men chose the path of restraint over the path of violence, and both men placed the survival of the nation above the survival of their own power.

===Historical assessment===

Modern historical assessment of Shigetoki has been shaped by the declassification of the ''Mitsukoji'' in 2016 and the subsequent reassessment of the official chronicle's biases and omissions. The ''Mitsukoji'' portrays Shigetoki as a man of quiet competence who rose to an impossible challenge—not through military genius or charismatic leadership, but through the disciplined application of institutional knowledge and moral courage.

The 2016 release of the [[Honda Letters]]—the personal correspondence of Shogun Honda Shigetomo—confirmed the ''Mitsukoji''{{'}}s account of the Kinzō Incident and validated the chronicle's transcription of the bloodstained letter. This confirmation has, in turn, reinforced the historical assessment of Shigetoki's decision: the amnesty for the Ii clan was not merely political pragmatism but the fulfillment of his brother's dying wish, made possible by the mentorship of Ikemoto Yousuke and the moral authority of Emperor Daigo.

Historians have also noted the contrast between Shigetoki's governance style and that of his successor. Where Shigetoki ruled through consensus, institutions, and the disciplined application of mercy, Shigemasa ruled through fear, purges, and the systematic dismantling of the daimyos' autonomy. The Pax Honda was the product of both approaches—Shigetoki's mercy established the peace, and Shigemasa's terror maintained it—but the question of which approach was more essential to the peace's survival remains a subject of historiographical debate.

==In popular culture==

Shigetoki frequently appears in media dealing with the Kinzō Incident, usually portrayed as the voice of reason contrasting with Shigetomo's rage. His scholarly, deliberate character serves as a dramatic foil to the volatile, charismatic figures of his father and brother.

In the acclaimed [[Taisen drama|Taisen Drama]] ''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]'' (1983), which achieved a '''90.4% audience rating''' for its final episode—the highest in Sanese television history—Shigetoki was portrayed as a deeply conflicted man forced to carry the weight of an empire. The drama's depiction of Shigetoki's assumption of power following the Kinzō Incident emphasized the loneliness of the man who had to choose between vengeance and peace while the nation demanded blood.

He also plays a major supporting role in the 2008 series ''Genna Revolt'', which focuses on the political challenges of transitioning from a wartime to a peacetime government. The series explores the institutional reforms of Shigetoki's reign and the tensions between his conciliatory approach and the hardline faction that would eventually coalesce around his nephew Shigemasa.

The 2014 Taisen drama ''Honda Tenno'', which dramatized Emperor Shirakawa's life, portrayed Shigetoki as a quiet youth in the background of his father's court—a deliberate choice that emphasized the contrast between the warrior-emperor and the scholar-bureaucrat son who would ultimately save what the warrior had built.

The character of Ikemoto Yousuke, Shigetoki's mentor and the last surviving Pillar, has appeared in several recent historical novels and manga, reflecting growing popular interest in the "fourth Pillar" whose mentorship made the Pax Honda possible. The 2023 novel ''The Last Pillar'' (最後の柱) by Sanese author Kurosawa Ichiban is a fictionalized account of Yousuke's relationship with Shigetoki, drawing on the declassified ''Mitsukoji'' archives to dramatize the transmission of the mercy lesson from the founding generation to the man who would act on it.

